# Calibrate pen and blank-sheet coordinates

## Scope and existing evidence

Measure usable area, origin, axis orientation, scale, skew, pen-up height,
contact height, speed, acceleration, page clamping, and boundary clearance.

Current executable evidence now freezes those calibration measurements as one
transport-neutral record bound to an identified device and explicit measurement
evidence. Coordinate transform, pen-motion envelope, and blank-sheet
registration remain separate grouped values while all units, tolerances, axis
conventions, calibration algorithms, coordinate transforms, limit validation,
dry-run admission, and physical arming remain open hardware-boundary work.

## Finite one-device blank-sheet calibration recipe

**Inputs:** an identified, hypothetical `D-01` single-pen device, one flat
A4 blank sheet, repeatable sheet clamps, selected pen/tool, axis and units
metadata, fiducial marks, measured tool contact/lift observations, and a dry
run result. **Outputs:** a device-bound, revisioned transform, pen offset,
measured usable rectangle and uncertainty ledger, or an explicit refusal.
This recipe does not establish that any specific model has been calibrated.

**Registration sequence:** (1) secure a flat sheet without a tool motion;
(2) mark paper fiducials at `(20,20)`, `(190,20)`, `(20,277) mm` and a
fourth check at `(190,277) mm`; (3) record device-coordinate readings and
fit one affine 2D mapping with explicit x/y direction, scale, rotation and
skew; (4) compare the fourth point against prediction, rather than fitting
it; (5) measure contact and lift clearance, pen-axis offset, usable region,
clamp exclusion and speed/acceleration envelopes separately; (6) run a
read-only proposed-path dry run and do not arm or plot automatically.

**Worked arithmetic, not measurements:** suppose the first three registered
fiducials read `(0,0)`, `(170,0)`, `(0,257) mm` in device coordinates, so
this example maps page `(x,y)` to device `(x-20,y-20)`. An independently
observed check point `(170.3,257.2)` differs from its predicted `(170,257)`
by about `0.36 mm`. Declare a `0.5 mm` hypothetical registration tolerance;
this example is inside it. If a pen mark is hypothetically displaced
`(+0.4,-0.2) mm` in device axes, subtract this offset from planned contact
coordinates and keep its observation uncertainty `±0.2 mm` explicit. No
actual machine origin, contact height, scale, offset or safe motion follows
from these invented arithmetic inputs.

**Calibration ledger:** carry device identity, firmware and tool identity;
page dimensions, four fiducial readings, axis signs, transform matrix and
units, max residual and uncertainty, measured pen-up/contact clearances,
probed speed/acceleration limits, clamp geometry, final usable rectangle,
operator, time and method. A missing measured field is `Unknown`, not zero;
any command envelope must wait for real measurements and explicit arming.

**Refusal:** a fourth-point residual `0.8 mm` against the declared `0.5 mm`
limit, an inverted or ambiguous axis, missing contact/lift observation, a
clamp inside the proposed path, changed tool, or an unknown boundary returns
`CalibrationNotAdmitted`. Preserve prior calibration and issue no movement.
Recalibration is needed for an altered sheet mount, pen or mechanical setup.

**Assumptions and risks:** the typed record groups transform, pen envelope
and paper registration but performs no fit or physical test. Skew, backlash,
pen flexibility, clamp drift and surface height variation can invalidate an
affine fit. This design is not a manufacturer procedure, device safety
certification, or evidence that a writer is connected.

## Acceptance decision

- **Basis:** theoretical one-device recipe, 2026-11-02; no measurements taken.
- **Delivered:** finite four-point registration and pen-offset sequence,
  bounded residual calculation, unknown-field policy and refusal conditions.
- **Unavailable:** measured device calibration, verified speed/contact
  clearance, hardware arming, or safe plot execution.
